<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- Needed from Phase 2 for the hub and the cloud. Phase 1 loads only
         bundled assets and reaches the network for nothing at all. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
        NOT OPTIONAL, AND NOT COSMETIC. Without this, Play would refuse to
        serve the app to the devices it was built for.

        Declaring RECORD_AUDIO IMPLIES a hardware requirement. Per
        developer.android.com/guide/topics/manifest/uses-feature-element,
        Table 2, RECORD_AUDIO implies android.hardware.microphone, and
        "uses-feature declarations always take precedence over features
        implied by the permissions in table 2". With no explicit declaration
        Play treats the microphone as REQUIRED and filters out every device
        that has none.

        Hestia's stated primary form factor is a WALL PANEL. Cheap mic-less
        tablets, most Android TV configurations and many Chromebooks would
        simply never see the listing, and nobody sees an error either: the
        app just is not there. Athena is optional in the product and was
        mandatory in the distribution filter.

        Same reasoning for the nearby-devices and local-network side.
        Discovery is a convenience with a manual-entry fallback, so a device
        without Wi-Fi scanning hardware must still be allowed to install.

        (Note for future edits: a double hyphen is ILLEGAL inside an XML
        comment and breaks the manifest merger with a bare "Error parsing"
        that names no line. This has cost five separate attempts on this file.)
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.microphone" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.wifi" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.touchscreen" android:required="false" />

    <!-- THE PERMISSION THIS ENTIRE APP DEPENDS ON.

         Verified against developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/local-network-permission
         on 2026-10-12 rather than assumed:

           "The ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK permission is required only if your app
            targets Android 17 (SDK 37) or higher. If your app targets SDK 36
            or lower, local network access is implicitly granted using the
            INTERNET permission."

         We target 37, so it is required. It gates far more than discovery:
         "Traffic to and from a local network address requires local network
         access permission", across "all networking APIs", OkHttp included.
         Without it the app cannot reach the hub at all. Not to poll, not to
         arm, not to unlock a door.

         WebView traffic inherits the host app's state, so the dashboard's own
         fetches are covered by this too.

         On Android 16 the restriction is opt-in and this is a no-op, which is
         why hub access worked on a Galaxy A16 before the permission existed.
         From Android 17 it is enforced by default. That gap is a trap: it
         works on every device we own today, and breaks on all of them once
         they update.

         NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES is the Android 16 stand-in named by the same page
         ("temporarily implemented as NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES"), and carries
         neverForLocation so it does not drag in a location prompt for a
         feature that has nothing to do with location. -->
    <!-- Athena. Requested at the moment the user taps Athena, never at
         startup, and granted to the WebView only for audio and only for the
         bundled page (see MainActivity.onPermissionRequest).

         Declaring it is not enough on its own: a WebView denies getUserMedia
         outright unless onPermissionRequest is overridden, which is why Athena
         failed on a real phone with a permission the user was never asked for
         and could not grant anywhere. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />

    <!-- Needed WITH RECORD_AUDIO, not instead of it, and its absence is the
         most likely cause of the failure that followed granting the mic:

           permission android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO: granted
           [Athena] Mic permission denied: Could not start audio source

         That is not a permission error. The permission was held, the device's
         global mic toggle was off, and nothing else held the input (checked
         via dumpsys on the phone). Chromium's audio capture adjusts the audio
         mode and takes focus when it opens a source, which this permission
         governs, and an AudioRecord that fails to initialise surfaces to
         JavaScript as exactly that message.

         It is a normal permission, so it is granted at install and prompts
         nobody. Declared here because every WebView-based app doing
         getUserMedia declares both. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS" />

    <!-- Runtime from Android 13. Requested when the user turns push on, never
         at startup: a denial is sticky, so the question gets one asking and it
         should follow the reason. Same rule as the microphone and the local
         network permission. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK" />
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES"
        android:usesPermissionFlags="neverForLocation" />

    <!--
        usesCleartextTraffic is TRUE below. Read this before changing it.

        It was false, which was better, but it made CameraProxy impossible. The
        flag is enforced by the HTTP stack, not only the WebView, so OkHttp
        threw UnknownServiceException before a socket opened. A household whose
        cameras answer on plain http at the camera's own address (UniFi
        anonymous snapshot) saw black tiles with no way to fix them. That is a
        capability a browser gave them and the app took away.

        Android's network security config can permit cleartext per domain, but
        it is static XML fixed at build time, while camera addresses are
        per household and set at runtime. There is no way to express "any
        private address", so scoping it was not an option.

        WHAT HOLDS THE LINE NOW, in code rather than as a side effect of one
        global flag:
          1. HubTrust.storedOrigin() returns null for a non-https origin, so
             neither HubClient nor HubProxy can relay in the clear.
          2. HubClient.configure() refuses a non-https hub outright.
          3. CloudClient.request() asserts its own origin is https.
          4. CameraProxy fetches only registered camera origins whose host
             resolves to a private address.

        Those four replaced one flag. Do not drop any of them on the grounds
        that the platform handles it. It no longer does.

        The WebView is unaffected: it keeps the platform default of
        MIXED_CONTENT_NEVER_ALLOW, so the page still cannot load http itself.
        Only CameraProxy can, behind the checks above.
    -->
    <!--
        allowBackup="false" is kept, but it is NOT the control. Android's docs:
        for apps targeting API 31+, "On devices from some device manufacturers,
        you can't disable device-to-device migration of your app's files."
        targetSdk is 37 and the test device is Samsung. So the two rule files
        below do the actual work, and they cover different API ranges:
        dataExtractionRules from API 31, fullBackupContent for API 30 and below
        (minSdk is 26). Neither substitutes for the other. See
        res/xml/backup_rules.xml for what would otherwise travel, and why the
        pinned certificate hash is the sharpest part of it.
    -->
    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/full_backup_content"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Hestia"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="true">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Hestia">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!-- exported=false: nothing outside the app may start it. FCM delivers
             through Play Services, which binds it by permission, not by an
             intent any app could send. -->
        <service
            android:name=".HestiaMessaging"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.firebase.MESSAGING_EVENT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!-- Used only when a message arrives WITHOUT the app choosing a
             channel, which should not happen because the backend sends
             data-only. Declared so that if one ever slips through, it lands in
             the quieter channel rather than Firebase's own default, which is
             created at IMPORTANCE_DEFAULT with a name the user has no way to
             recognise in their notification settings. -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_channel_id"
            android:value="hestia_events" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_icon"
            android:resource="@drawable/ic_notification" />
    </application>

</manifest>
